Using $40.5$ femtobarns of data collected by the LHCb experiment at 13 TeV, this study aims to determine the LHCb experiment's sensitivity in identifying the sign of the $c$-quark helicity amplitude in the process $B_s^0 	o D_s^{*-} \mu^+ 
u_{\mu}$. A differential decay rate form factor, calculated through perturbative QCD, facilitates this investigation. Based on current data, the uncertainty in the $c$ quark helicity amplitude remains substantial, primarily due to substantial uncertainties in the input parameters and the need for additional theoretical developments to better understand the $D_s^*$ form factor. To further clarify the $c$ quark helicity amplitude, future studies could focus on enhancing the precision of the $D_s^*$ form factor or the strong interaction constant $\alpha_s$ used in this analysis. To achieve this, researchers suggest possible approaches, such as utilizing reverse kinematics, exploring the $B_s^0 	o D_s \pi^{-}  ar{
u}_{\mu}$ process, or employing alternative methods to determine the $c$ quark helicity amplitude. To facilitate these investigations, this study makes available for the first time the results of extensive simulations detailing the $B_s^0 	o D_s^{*-} \mu^+ 
u_{\mu}$ process, including estimates of hadronization effects and form factor parameters. To enhance future understanding of this process, researchers advise developing better theoretical models of the $D_s^*$ form factor and investigating methods to directly determine the $c$ quark helicity amplitude. To facilitate this development, this study makes available for the first time the results of extensive simulations detailing the $B_s^0 \ to D_s \pi^{-}  ar{
